# Prediction accuracy

Your loop predicts your glucose, and it makes its decisions from these
predictions. This page compares the predictions with the readings that followed
them, at 30, 60, 90 and 120 minutes ahead.

The [Loop Analysis overview](/docs/reports/loop-analysis) tells you about the
filters, net loop correction and how to read the charts. They are the same on
all five pages.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-predictions" />

- **Bias** is the average of _prediction minus reading_. A positive bias means
  that the predictions were too high. A negative bias means that they were too
  low.
- **MAE** (mean absolute error) is the average size of the error, in the two
  directions.
- **n** is the number of predictions that the report compared.

The report pairs a prediction only with a reading within 2.5 minutes of the
predicted time. If there is no reading that close, the prediction is unmatched.
The report does not use a horizon whose reading can still arrive.

## Why only 30 and 60 minutes are about your settings

A prediction assumes that nothing else will occur. But the loop continues to
change insulin after the prediction. After two hours, glucose also shows all of
these changes. So only the **bias at 30 and 60 minutes** gives information
about your settings. For example, if predictions after meals are usually too
high, speak to your diabetes care team. The longer horizons are for information only.

## Interrupted predictions

If you logged carbs or gave a manual bolus after a prediction and before its
horizon, the prediction did not know about it. These horizons are
**interrupted**. The report does not use them in a figure. Food that you did
not log does not interrupt a prediction.

## Curves and contexts

Loop makes one prediction. AAPS, Trio, iAPS and OpenAPS make a number of
predictions. Each one assumes something different: **IOB** (insulin only),
**zero temp** (if basal stops), **COB** (carbs absorb as entered) and **UAM**
(unannounced meal, from the rise in glucose). The report checks each curve
only in the contexts where the algorithm uses it. For example, IOB and zero
temp are checked after a correction or when fasting. COB is checked after a
meal.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-prediction-detail" />

The **By hour and context** box shows the bias of one curve at each hour of the
day and in each context.

Two curves select the predictions that the report can check:

- The algorithm makes **UAM** only when glucose increases faster than insulin
  alone predicts, or when carbs are absorbing. So the report measures UAM
  accuracy only at these times.
- The algorithm removes the end of the **zero temp** curve where the curve
  increases above the target. So its long horizons come only from predictions
  that did not increase above the target.

If your access does not include treatments, the report hides the curves that
it checks only in some contexts: IOB, zero temp and COB. The page names these
curves.
The contexts come from treatments.

